6) Next Greater Element [Monotonic Stack]

Monotonic Stack : When we use stack to store element in a specific order(either increasing or decreasing) then we call monotonic stack.

Brute Force Approach : class Solution {
    public ArrayList<Integer> nextLargerElement(int[] arr) {
        ArrayList<Integer> ans = new ArrayList<>();
        for(int i=0;i<arr.length;i++)
        {
            boolean flag = false;
            for(int j=i+1;j<arr.length;j++)
            {
                if(arr[j]>arr[i])
                {
                    flag = true;
                    ans.add(arr[j]);
                    break;
                }
            }
            if(flag==false)
            {
                ans.add(-1);
            }
        }
        return ans;
    }
}

T.C. -> O(N^2)
S.C. -> O(N) but it is used to store the ans, so this can't be improved.

Optimal Approach : class Solution {
    public ArrayList<Integer> nextLargerElement(int[] arr) {
        ArrayList<Integer> ans = new ArrayList<>();
        Stack<Integer> st = new Stack<>();
        for(int i=arr.length-1;i>=0;i--)
        {
            while(!st.isEmpty() && st.peek()<=arr[i])
            {
                st.pop();
            }
            if(st.isEmpty()) ans.add(-1);
            else ans.add(st.peek());
            st.push(arr[i]);
        }
        Collections.reverse(ans);
        return ans;
    }
}

T.C. -> O(2N)
S.C. -> O(N) + O(N)

